#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# Keep account figures out of commit messages.
#
# This is not hypothetical. The only real leak this repo has had was a commit
# *body* that stated an actual net worth and holdings value — the code in that
# commit was clean, and every scanner that looks at diffs would have passed it.
# It was caught because the commit had not been pushed yet.
#
# Commit messages are as public as code, and rewriting one that has been pushed
# means a force-push that does not un-publish anything anyway.
#
# Warns rather than blocks. Balances are legitimately discussable as counts and
# ratios, the pattern cannot tell "$2,104.99 in holdings" from "$5 test charge",
# and a hook that blocks on ambiguity gets bypassed reflexively. A warning that
# names the line is enough to stop the mistake being made accidentally.

set -uo pipefail

msg_file="$1"
[ -f "$msg_file" ] || exit 0

YELLOW=$'\033[0;33m'; RESET=$'\033[0m'

# Ignore comment lines — git's own template is full of them.
body=$(grep -v '^#' "$msg_file" || true)

# Currency amounts with a decimal or thousands separator. A bare "$5" is almost
# always an example; "$2,104.99" is almost always real.
if hits=$(printf '%s\n' "$body" | grep -nE '\$[0-9]{1,3}([,][0-9]{3})+(\.[0-9]{2})?|\$[0-9]+\.[0-9]{2}'); then
    printf '%s\n' "$YELLOW" >&2
    printf 'WARNING: this commit message contains what looks like a real balance:%s\n' "$RESET" >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$hits" >&2
    printf '%s\n' "$YELLOW" >&2
    cat >&2 <<'EOF'
This repo is public. Commit messages are as readable as code, and a pushed
message cannot be unpublished by rewriting it.

State verification results as counts, ratios or "idempotent on re-run" rather
than as balances. If the figure is illustrative rather than yours, it is fine —
this is a warning, not a block, and the commit is proceeding.
EOF
    printf '%s\n' "$RESET" >&2
fi

exit 0
